EXT. ROPE BRIDGE OVER GORGE - DAY

A plank bridge slung across a mile-wide chasm in the Andes. Wind howls. Clouds drift below, bone white. The planks are gray and gapped. At the middle, four hikers stand frozen on the sagging span, packs on their backs.

JOSEPHINE "JOJO" TAN, thirties, leads, one hand on the rope rail. EMEKA OBI, forties, a doctor, follows. DARIUS WHITTAKER, twenties, a photographer, clutches a camera case to his chest. SOLVEIG ENGSTROM, sixties, a retired geologist, brings up the rear, step by careful step.

JOJO
Everyone stay on the center line.

DARIUS
There is no center line, Jojo.

JOJO
Then pretend.

Wind slaps the bridge. The whole span lurches. Emeka grips the rail. Darius shrieks. Solveig plants her trekking poles and stands like a tree.

SOLVEIG
(muttering)
Don't look down, don't look down, don't look down.

EMEKA
Solveig, can you keep moving?

SOLVEIG
I can keep moving. I choose not to.

A plank cracks and drops away into the cloud. Jojo leaps over the gap and lands badly. Darius lunges after her, Emeka catches his pack strap, and for a moment all three are a tangle at the edge of a missing board, the camera case swinging wild.

JOJO
Hold! Hold!

DARIUS
I'm holding!

EMEKA
You're holding the camera!

Darius looks down at his own hand, clamped on the case, and his other hand, empty, flailing. He drops the case. It tumbles, small and square, into the white. He grabs the rope.

DARIUS
(whispering)
That was ten years of work.

SOLVEIG
(to the others)
We cross now. Single file. Quickly.

JOJO
Agreed.

They move. The bridge sways like a hammock. Jojo steps across the gap first, then Emeka, then Darius, who is weeping silently, then Solveig, steady as a metronome, until they all stand together on solid rock on the far side.

Nobody speaks. Solveig lowers herself onto a boulder. Jojo laughs, once, a short astonished sound. Emeka puts a hand on Darius's shoulder, and Darius, finally, nods.
